On Fri, 16 Oct 2026 22:12:31 -0700, Pat Maddox wrote:
> I'm convinced by the git approach. It will be a lot easier for me to
> track work going on in the list if it's all in a thread.
>
> Yep, get to work on whatever changes need to be made to support
> this.

Done. It took two series:

"[PATCH 0/3] Thread each revision under its first version" [1]
changes the tooling and the docs. format-patch.sh already gets
v(N-1) from --previous. It now follows In-Reply-To from there back
to v1 and passes that as --in-reply-to, so the agent never picks the
reply target. A revision that was posted in a thread of its own,
under the old convention, ends the walk, so its next version joins
that thread. lab-process-mail's prompt, doc/mail-workflow.md,
patch-triage, and freebsd-port now describe the new threading. The
"Link to v(N-1)" line stays, since readers and the superseded tag
both use it.

The series builds on "[PATCH v2 0/4] Require an interdiff on revised
series" [2], which is not applied yet and is what adds --previous.

"[PATCH v4] notmuch: Tag series a later version replaces" [3] fixes
the superseded tag. v3, the version you are running, tags the whole
thread a Link line names. Once revisions share v1's thread, the
first v2 would mark the whole thread superseded, including v2
itself. v4 tags only the replaced version and the replies below it.
It applies to b/dev on its own. Please apply it before, or together
with, the threading series. I posted it as a reply to v3, the way
the new convention handles a series that started in its own thread.

One thing on b/dev also assumes one thread per version, and I left
it alone. "mail-file superseded thread:<old> id:<new>" moves the
whole thread into superseded/ and posts a breadcrumb reply there.
Under the new threading, that would file the current version away
together with the old ones. Since the tag now tracks supersession
automatically, and you dropped the breadcrumb reply in "Re: [PATCH
0/3] notmuch: Tag superseded series and hide them" [4], I recommend
removing the superseded verb from mail-file, its man page, tests,
and the Emacs binding. The alternative is to teach it to move a
single version, but that duplicates what the tag already does. Say
which you want and I will send it.

